> Recent estimates of economic loss and damage due to climate change vary significantly, reflecting a range of assumptions and methodologies. Markandya & González-Eguino (2018) use an integrated assessment modeling approach to estimate that non-Annex I countries experienced losses ranging from $179 to $671 billion per year as of 2020. In contrast, Songwe et al. (2022) anticipate losses based on engineering estimates to reach $150 to $300 billion annually by 2030. Examining extreme weather events, Newman & Noy (2023) attribute $53 billion per year to market damages and an additional $90 billion per year to losses valued through the Value of Statistical Life (VSL) methodology. Meanwhile, my own work (Rising, 2023) based on a meta-analysis offers a more conservative estimate of $4 billion per year in damages by 2023. Together, these studies underscore the substantial and varied economic impacts of climate change, highlighting the urgent need for tailored mitigation and adaptation strategies.
